---
import DocsLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Level Sequence Preview — MHA Manager"
  description="Export a playable, self-lit Level Sequence per job and review the solve in Sequencer without building a shot."
  currentSlug="level-sequence-preview"
>

  <div class="doc-header">
    <h1 class="doc-title">Level Sequence Preview</h1>
    <p class="doc-lead">
      Reviewing a solve normally means building a shot: drop a MetaHuman in a level, wire the
      animation, light it, aim a camera. Turn on Level Sequence export and each job writes that shot
      for you — framed, lit, and playable in whatever level you happen to have open.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">What gets exported</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      <strong>Export LevelSequence</strong> is on by default in
      <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/global-settings">Global Settings</a>, so every job adds
      a Level Sequence to its outputs beside the Performance and AnimSequence. Three things make it
      reviewable on its own:
    </p>
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="ls-row header">
        <span>Feature</span>
        <span>What it does</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-row">
        <span><strong>Framed camera</strong></span>
        <span>The camera is placed to frame the face and given a 50mm portrait lens with focus set to the subject. Without footage to match, the engine falls back to a 10mm lens, which reads as a fisheye on a face at conversational distance.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-row">
        <span><strong>Pinned exposure</strong></span>
        <span>MetaHuman exposure settings are written onto the camera itself, so the face is correctly exposed no matter which level the sequence is opened in.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-row">
        <span><strong>Baked-in lighting</strong></span>
        <span>The chosen lighting rig's lights are copied into the sequence as spawnables. The sequence lights its own face instead of borrowing whatever the current level has.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Face binding</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      The engine binds a face to an exported Level Sequence only for stereo takes — where it uses
      the take's own rigged identity. An audio sequence would otherwise play dialogue over an empty
      frame, and a mono one its plate with nothing animated in front of it, so for those two the
      plugin binds a face itself, in order of preference:
    </p>
    <ol class="numbered-list">
      <li>
        <strong>A MetaHuman actor Blueprint</strong> you nominate in settings — the better target,
        because the engine spawns it and bakes an editable Face Control Rig into the sequence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A face mesh</strong>, bound to a plain animation track playing the AnimSequence the
        job already exported. Leave it empty and a skeletal mesh built on that AnimSequence's own
        skeleton is found automatically.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout callout-warn">
      An explicit face mesh on a <em>different</em> skeleton is rejected rather than bound. The
      shipped face archetypes carry identical bones under different Skeleton assets, so a mismatched
      pair looks correct and evaluates to nothing — a silent, still face is worse than a clear skip.
    </div>
    <p class="section-desc">
      A mono sequence that gets a face drops the footage plate: the engine adds it as a standalone
      plane in front of the take's own camera, and the bound face is reframed at the origin, so the
      plate would only sit in shot as a stray billboard. Only a face-bound sequence gets the
      lighting rig — there is nothing for it to light otherwise.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Choosing the lighting rig</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      The rig is picked by name from Epic's MetaHuman lighting environments —
      <strong>Portrait</strong> by default, with Studio, Split, Fireside, Moonlight, Tungsten,
      RedLantern, and TextureBooth alongside it. The list is discovered from the installed content
      rather than hardcoded, so environments a future engine adds show up on their own.
    </p>
    <p class="section-desc">
      The rigs' shared companion level (the backdrop and its post-process grade) is deliberately left
      out of the sequence: the preview action opens the sequence against that level anyway, and a
      second copy inside the sequence would double it up. A project without the MetaHuman Character
      plugin content exports an <strong>unlit</strong> sequence rather than failing — the camera's
      own exposure pin still applies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Preview in Studio</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Right-click any finished job with a Level Sequence artifact and choose
      <strong>Preview in Studio</strong>. The sequence opens in Sequencer, ready to scrub.
    </p>
    <p class="section-desc">
      With <strong>Load Studio Backdrop When Previewing LevelSequence</strong> on (the default), the
      editor loads Epic's clean MetaHuman backdrop level first for a controlled background. Loading
      it <strong>replaces the current level</strong>, so the editor offers unsaved work up for saving
      first — declining that prompt cancels the preview rather than half-performing it. Turn the
      setting off to open the sequence in whatever level is already loaded, with no map switch. A
      project without the MetaHuman Character plugin content always opens in the current level, and
      the job says so.
    </p>
    <div class="callout callout-note">
      Because the lighting travels inside the sequence, the backdrop is only ever a background. The
      face stays lit either way.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Wwise dialogue</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      A Wwise-sourced job's sequence carries a Wwise event track instead of a sound wave track, so
      the dialogue plays through the sound engine. See
      <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/wwise-audio">Wwise Audio</a> — including the audio
      routing setting that silences sound wave tracks entirely.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Driving it from a script</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      <code>preview_level_sequence(job_id)</code> performs the same action from an MCP client,
      including the backdrop load and its save prompt. See
      <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/mcp-toolset">MCP Toolset</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .doc-header { margin-bottom: 40px; }

  .doc-title {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .doc-lead {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 620px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .doc-section { margin-bottom: 48px; }

  .section-title {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .section-desc {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }

  .section-desc strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .field-list {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .ls-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 2.8fr;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 11px 16px;
    font-size: 0.825rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    align-items: start;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .ls-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  .ls-row.header {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .ls-row strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .numbered-list {
    padding-left: 20px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .numbered-list li {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
    padding-left: 4px;
  }

  .numbered-list li strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .callout {
    padding: 14px 18px;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-top: 16px;
    border-left: 3px solid;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .callout-warn { background: color-mix(in srgb, #f0a500 8%, transparent); border-color: #f0a500; }
  .callout-note { background: var(--bg-surface); border-color: var(--border); }
  .callout strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
  .callout em { color: var(--text); font-style: italic; }

  a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .ls-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
  }
</style>
